{
  // **THE NAME AND THE DOMAINS MOVED TOGETHER, AND THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE POINT — MEASURED 2026-10-08.**
  //
  // The paragraph this replaces is why the order matters. On 2026-10-05 this file was renamed to `summrise-gate`
  // while the console's CUSTOM DOMAINS still pointed at `vale-gate`, so nine days of `./scripts/build.sh gateway`
  // updated a worker nothing routed to and the live console stayed frozen at its 2026-09-28 build while nine
  // commits touched `gateway/src` or `gateway/ui`. 315c1ef0 measured it, and the check that settled it was the
  // DOMAIN TABLE — a request count cannot say who served a request when both implementations answer the same bytes.
  //
  //     before   api.saisi.online -> vale-gate          ai.saisi.online -> vale-gate
  //     after    api.saisi.online -> summrise-gate      ai.saisi.online -> summrise-gate
  //
  // **`vale-gate` IS STILL DEPLOYED, AND DELETING IT IS NOT CLEANUP — IT IS DATA LOSS.** It owns the two Durable
  // Object classes this worker binds below with `script_name`: a Durable Object's storage is keyed by the SCRIPT
  // that defines its class, so a new owner starts EMPTY, and one of the two holds user data (`RouteDO`:
  // `route:{uid}` → the model that user routes to). The old worker is therefore the DO owner AND the rollback:
  // moving the two custom domains back is one API call, and it needs no redeploy of `vale-gate`, whose deployed
  // code is unchanged. (`RouteDO`'s own HTTP API — GET/PUT/DELETE `/route?uid=` — is what a later migration to a
  // self-owned class would read and write; that is a scripted step, not a rename.)
  "name": "summrise-gate",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-01",
  "workers_dev": false,
  "observability": { "enabled": true, "head_sampling_rate": 1 },
  "kv_namespaces": [
    {
      "binding": "KEYS",
      "id": "a1fb10da621b411caeda6d98c060bbea"
    }
  ],
  // ── THE CUTOVER'S ONE LINK: /v1/* IS SERVED BY THE RUST WORKER ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // **A SERVICE BINDING RATHER THAN A ZONE ROUTE, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT.** The plan said "two zone routes on the
  // console hosts"; adding one and watching Cloudflare's own analytics for the day showed the route was INERT —
  //
  //     vale-gate       550 requests
  //     vale-gate-wasm    3 requests   (the three were this box's `wrangler dev --remote` probes)
  //
  // — because these hostnames are Worker CUSTOM DOMAINS, and a custom domain serves the whole host: `api.saisi.online`
  // is the console page (200, text/html, 1,391 B) as well as the API, so repointing it would take the console away with
  // it. A service binding keeps ONE hostname, one DNS record and one Access policy, leaves the console's own routes
  // alone, and gives the cutover a one-line rollback: delete this binding (or the `WASM_GATE` branch in
  // `src/index.ts`) and the next deploy serves /v1 from TypeScript again.
  // **BOUND TO `vale-gate`, WHICH IS WHAT KEEPS THE STATE — SEE THE NOTE ABOVE THE NAME.**
  //
  // `script_name` is the documented cross-script binding, and this account already runs one:
  // `gateway/wasm/wrangler.jsonc` binds BREAKER to `vale-gate`, and its deploy reports
  // `env.BREAKER (BreakerDO, defined in vale-gate)`. Declaring the classes HERE instead would give this worker a
  // SECOND, EMPTY RouteDO — and RouteDO is the one with user data in it. The classes stay exported from
  // `src/index.ts`, because that source is what `vale-gate` runs; they are simply not this script's to define.
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      {
        "name": "BREAKER",
        "class_name": "BreakerDO",
        "script_name": "vale-gate"
      },
      {
        "name": "ROUTE",
        "class_name": "RouteDO",
        "script_name": "vale-gate"
      }
    ]
  },
  // **THE HISTORY IS `vale-gate`'S OWN AGAIN, AND THE PARAGRAPH THAT USED TO SIT HERE WAS THE BUG'S OWN
  // DESCRIPTION.** It said the history was "shorter than `vale-gate`'s, and it has to be", because the 2026-10-05
  // rename to `summrise-gate` could not apply `v4-delete-plugin-hub` — `deleted_classes` only applies to a worker
  // that HAD the class, and the NEW worker never had `PluginHubDO`. **THE RENAME WAS THE MISTAKE, NOT THE
  // MIGRATION**: the console's custom domains kept pointing at `vale-gate`, so the new worker was deployed to and
  // never routed to, and the consequence the old paragraph accepted as a trade — "the Durable Objects start
  // EMPTY" — was a consequence of moving to a worker nobody used.
  //
  // Measured 2026-10-07 on the attempt to deploy this file to `vale-gate`: the API refused with
  // `Cannot apply new-sqlite-class migration to class 'BreakerDO' that is already depended on by existing Durable
  // Objects [code: 10074]` and warned that the published script's tag `v4-delete-plugin-hub` was missing here —
  // i.e. it tried to RE-APPLY the whole history because the list did not match. **IT FAILED BEFORE CHANGING THE
  // SCRIPT**, which is the safe direction, and the tag is restored below.
  // **NO `migrations` KEY, AND THAT IS THE FIX RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.** The history below was restored in the
  // attempt to make this file deployable to `vale-gate` again, and BOTH shapes failed at the API:
  //
  //     with  v1-breaker, v3-route          -> Cannot apply new-sqlite-class migration to class 'BreakerDO' that is
  //                                            already depended on by existing Durable Objects [code: 10074]
  //     with  v1-breaker, v3-route, v4-…    -> Cannot apply deleted_classes migration to non-existent class PluginHubDO
  //
  // The first warning said why: the published script's tag `v4-delete-plugin-hub` "was not found in your
  // wrangler.jsonc file ... **Applying all available migrations to the script**" — a list that does not match the
  // published one makes wrangler re-apply the WHOLE history, and every tag in it has already been applied.
  //
  // **A MIGRATION LIST IS FOR CHANGING CLASSES; THIS DEPLOY CHANGES NONE.** The bindings below are unchanged and
  // the classes are unchanged, so the deploy needs no migration at all — and Cloudflare keeps the published tag
  // (`v4-delete-plugin-hub`) as it is. A future class is added by writing a new tag HERE, which is the moment the
  // key earns its place:
  //
  //     "migrations": [ { "tag": "v5-…", "new_sqlite_classes": ["…"] } ]
  //
  // The history this file used to carry, for the record: `v1-breaker` (new_sqlite_classes BreakerDO),
  // `v3-route` (new_sqlite_classes RouteDO), `v4-delete-plugin-hub` (deleted_classes PluginHubDO).
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./public",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "not_found_handling": "none",
    "run_worker_first": true
  },
  // THE RELAY, BY SERVICE BINDING (ADR 0010 D6). The file relay is its own worker now
  // (`summrise-relay`), and the gateway reaches its /api/upload through this binding
  // rather than a same-zone fetch: Cloudflare is explicit that a Route cannot be the
  // target of a same-zone fetch() while a Custom Domain can — and the relay's DOWNLOAD
  // leg IS a Route (`/files/*`, which takes precedence over summrise-dist's Custom
  // Domain), so a fetch would be exactly the case the docs warn about. The binding also
  // takes the upload endpoint off the public internet: nothing but this worker can call
  // it, because `workers_dev` is false and no route is declared for it.
  // **TWO SERVICE BINDINGS NOW, AND THE SECOND IS THE CUTOVER'S.** `RELAY` is the file relay the console has always
  // had; `WASM_GATE` is the Rust front door — see the long note above `durable_objects` for why this is a binding
  // rather than the zone route the plan first described (the console hosts are CUSTOM DOMAINS, and a route on them
  // is inert: measured, 550 requests to `vale-gate` against 3 to `vale-gate-wasm`).
  // (no trailing comma: the CORS test parses this file with a strict JSON parser, and a JSONC-only trailing comma
  // is a syntax error there — measured, `SyntaxError: Unexpected token ']'`)
  "services": [
    { "binding": "RELAY", "service": "summrise-relay" },
    { "binding": "WASM_GATE", "service": "vale-gate-wasm" }
  ],
  "vars": {
    "CONSOLE_HOST": "ai.saisi.online,api.saisi.online",
    // Browser tool chain: cold-start chromium (~20s) + device SPA loading make the 30s default bound to time out
    "UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS": "120000",
    // Cloudflare Access SSO (option C): zero-password console login. The JWT
    // aud is the ai.saisi.online Access application's tag; ACCESS_ADMIN_EMAIL
    // binds the owner's mailbox to the seeded admin account.
    "ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN": "vale-saisi.cloudflareaccess.com",
    "ACCESS_AUD": "047684f18b6631d17a722235265b68d8ea558a7a5e40cb5f2b6e1ae5c9473a6b",
    "ACCESS_ADMIN_EMAIL": "13122231591@163.com",
    // Gateway-side image describing for text-only models: Command Code V4.1
    // Flash (GOAT plan). Descriptions are KV-cached per image. The V4
    // vision-exp describer was retired with the rest of the V4 line on
    // 2026-09-10 — V4.1 reads images natively, so it describes them too.
    "VISION_MODEL": "cm/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash",
    // Models that must NOT get the describe-first treatment on /v1/messages —
    // they read real Anthropic image blocks upstream. Entries match EITHER the
    // advertised id OR the wire name (isVisionCapable checks both):
    //   - deepseek-flash — zen/go's V4.1 lane slug (og/deepseek-v4.1-flash and
    //     the raw og/deepseek-flash spelling); live image test 2026-09-10 ok;
    //   - cm/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash — Command Code's V4.1: the
    //     chat/completions passthrough (which does NO pre-describing) read a
    //     240x80 PNG back verbatim on 2026-09-10, so the upstream model sees
    //     images natively.
    //   - qw/deepseek-v4.1-flash — the same V4.1 Flash on the Aliyun token
    //     plan (2026-09-16): an image_url request through the compatible-mode
    //     passthrough read a 320x100 PNG's text verbatim and reported
    //     image_tokens. Listed by ADVERTISED id; its wire name
    //     (deepseek-v4.1-flash) is deliberately NOT listed — a bare entry
    //     would also cover any future channel serving that same slug.
    //   - r4/deepseek-v4.1-flash — r4.codes (2026-09-20): an image_url request
    //     read a 320x100 PNG's text verbatim and reported
    //     prompt_tokens_details.image_tokens=202. Listed by ADVERTISED id for
    //     the same reason as qw/ above. NOTE the rest of the r4 catalog is NOT
    //     vision-capable (glm-5.3, deepseek-v4-pro, deepseek-v4-flash and
    //     glm-5.2 answered "I cannot see images" to the same probe), and the
    //     other three that DO see images (kimi-k3, glm-5.3-flash, qwen3.8-27b)
    //     are not registered in MODEL_REGISTRY — so this entry must NOT be
    //     widened to the bare wire slug, which would silently claim vision for
    //     the text-only seven.
    // The amd/ vision-exp entry left with the amd/ catalog (no V4.1 upstream).
    //   - og/mimo-v2.6-flash — zen/go (2026-09-22): an image_url request read a
    //     320x100 PNG's text verbatim, so the picture reaches the model instead
    //     of being described for it. ADVERTISED id only, as above.
    "VISION_CAPABLE_MODELS": "deepseek-flash, cm/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash, qw/deepseek-v4.1-flash, r4/deepseek-v4.1-flash, og/mimo-v2.6-flash"
  }
  // Summrise Gate — unified AI gateway + device console. Custom domains are added
  // in the dashboard (custom_domains API is OAuth-only).
  // Vars: committed non-sensitive defaults below; per-deploy overrides
  // (CONSOLE_HOST, API_HOST, OPENROUTER_PROXY_URL, US_PROXY) via dashboard /
  // `wrangler var put`.
  // Secrets (set via `wrangler secret put`): CLIENT_KEY, DEEPSEEK_API_KEY, OPENCODE_GO_API_KEY,
  // OPENROUTER_API_KEY, QWEN_API_KEY, CMD_API_KEY (worker-level Command Code
  // key, e.g. for the summrise-probe health check; per-user keys are BYOK via the console),
  // NVAPI_KEY, GMI_API_KEY, AMD_API_KEY (same pattern: worker-level key only
  // feeds /api/summrise-probe; real traffic uses the requesting user's own key),
  // R4_API_KEY (same pattern: worker-level r4.codes key for /api/summrise-probe;
  // real traffic uses the requesting user's own key),
  // ADMIN_PASSWORD,
  // SESSION_SECRET (high-entropy; decouples session signing from the admin password — set it!),
  // DO_AUTH (shared secret the main worker passes to BreakerDO/RouteDO to gate their /call)
}
